  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B051",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B009",
    "B048"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. B051 remains the most persuasive combination of fundamental physics, maturity, and delimited credit. The magic-wavelength lattice resolved the conflict between confining atoms and preserving the clock transition, creating a new precision regime that has been repeatedly realized and used for tests involving gravitation and fundamental physics. Katori and Ye remain a compact, credible pairing for the decisive concept and foundational realization. This is not primarily a prize for technical performance: the clocks make time and energy sensitive probes of the physical world.",
    "case_against": "The underlying achievement must be defined narrowly as the optical lattice-clock principle and its foundational realization, rather than optical clocks generally or later record-setting refinements. The supplied record also does not independently settle every detail of the relative contributions of Katori and Ye. These are real attribution qualifications, although they are narrower than the difficulty of assigning a large collaboration-scale discovery to three people.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that EIT is a mature quantum-interference phenomenon with durable consequences for slow light, storage, nonlinear optics, and quantum interfaces, but questions whether Imamoglu belongs with Harris and Hau in a prize centered on the original achievement.",
        "response": "I agree that B009 remains my closest conceptual alternative: EIT is beautiful atomic physics and its experimental consequences demonstrate genuine maturity. Kroll's concern reinforces my preference for B051, however. A citation confined to the lattice-clock invention can recognize a two-person concept-and-realization chain without deciding whether later coherent-control developments belong within the original EIT discovery."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson presents circuit QED as a reproducible foundational quantum-optical regime, while identifying its possible overlap with the 2025 superconducting-circuit recognition as a central boundary problem.",
        "response": "I share Johansson's respect for a new controllable light-matter regime and for the coherence of the proposed theory-experiment chain. But the unresolved relationship to the recent superconducting-circuit recognition makes B023 less ready for a final award. B051 has a cleaner scientific boundary: trapping neutral atoms at a magic wavelength while retaining clock accuracy is distinct from the broader enterprise of optical frequency metrology."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "pearce-mark",
        "point": "Pearce emphasizes that IceCube opened neutrino astronomy through cumulative diffuse-flux and source evidence, while recognizing that its large collaboration is difficult to represent fairly by three individuals.",
        "response": "I accept that B058 is a profound observational alternative and that cumulative evidence is preferable to a single striking event. I nevertheless retain B051 because its fundamental reach is now experimentally mature and its individual credit is substantially more tractable. This is not a judgment based on the number of supporters, but on the ability to make a precise award for a clearly identified advance."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the historical record can delimit Katori's and Ye's respective foundational contributions with sufficient precision for the proposed two-person allocation.",
      "Whether the committee judges the demonstrated fundamental uses of lattice clocks sufficiently central relative to their metrological and technological importance.",
      "Whether B009's original EIT achievement can support a comparably precise allocation that excludes later extensions."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No conflict with Katori or Ye is apparent from the supplied profile. My long-standing collaborations in attosecond physics concern different people and achievements and do not alter this assessment."
  }
}
